1	#!/usr/bin/env bun
2	/**
3	 * update_atomaton.ts — take a newer template release onto this tree.
4	 *
5	 * This is the other face of the machinery the notice serves. The notice (see
6	 * `notify_new_release.ts`) computes a plan and renders it read-only; this script
7	 * runs the same plan for real, on the checkout the workflow ran it in:
8	 *
9	 *     plan → apply to the tree → branch → commit → push → pull request
10	 *        → no conflict: request the auto-merge, close the notice
11	 *        → conflicts:   stop at the pull request, report where and why
12	 *
13	 * Nothing here decides policy. Whether a path is take/add/delete/merge is the
14	 * plan's answer (`domain/machinery/update-plan.ts`); whether a merge collides is
15	 * `git merge-file`'s measured answer (`lib/update-apply.ts`). A collision is not a
16	 * failure of the run: stopping at a pull request IS the design, and the run exits
17	 * green after reporting it. What is a failure is being unable to run at all — no
18	 * token, no manifest, no readable release — which exits red with the reason.
19	 *
20	 * ## Why the token, and why it is not the workflow's own
21	 *
22	 * The branch this pushes must run the repository's checks, and a push made with
23	 * the workflow's own `GITHUB_TOKEN` deliberately triggers none. The merge those
24	 * checks gate would then be merging unverified content. So the workflow that runs
25	 * this carries `ATOMATON_UPDATE_TOKEN`, a personal access token with repo scope,
26	 * and a run without it fails loudly and does nothing — a person who pressed the
27	 * button is standing there, and silence is not an answer.
28	 *
29	 * ## Why the stop is a pull request, and why the merge is requested, not done
30	 *
31	 * A pull request is the one place this repository already has everything a
32	 * resolution needs: visible diffs, checks that refuse broken content, and an agent
33	 * loop a person can address with a comment. The update asks for the auto-merge
34	 * (`gh pr merge --auto`) rather than merging outright, so the repository's own
35	 * checks stand between the release and the default branch even when nothing
36	 * collided — the plan being clean is the plan's claim, and the checks are what
37	 * makes it true.
38	 *
39	 * Usage:
